--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/system/devices/mctp-i2c-sensor.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,234 @@
+MCTP over I2C temperature sensor
+===============================
+
+The ``mctp-i2c-sensor`` device is a fixed-address temperature sensor using
+the MCTP SMBus/I2C transport (DSP0237). It receives SMBus block writes with
+command code ``0x0f`` and a PEC, and sends replies as separate I2C writes
+to the requester's address. The controller must support I2C target mode;
+the Aspeed controller provides this support.
+
+The implementation follows the reverse-controller approach in the
+`MCTP I2C responder proposal
+<https://patchew.org/QEMU/20260408085941.3837334-1-zhangjian.3032%40bytedance.com/>`_.
+
+The ``ast2600-evb`` machine creates one sensor on QEMU I2C bus 4 at the
+7-bit address ``0x1d``. Its QOM path is ``/machine/mctp-sensor``, its
+initial temperature is 35000 milli-degrees Celsius (35 degrees Celsius),
+and its initial EID is zero. The bus owner assigns an EID using the MCTP
+Control protocol. SMBus ARP is not implemented and address ``0x61`` is not
+used by the device. The I2C address and the MCTP EID are independent.
+
+Supported commands
+------------------
+
+MCTP Control supports Set Endpoint ID (including Force, Reset, and Set
+Discovered Flag operations), Get Endpoint ID, Get Endpoint UUID, Get
+MCTP Version Support, and Get Message Type Support. The latter advertises
+MCTP Control (``0x00``) and PLDM (``0x01``). The device is a simple
+endpoint with a dynamic EID; it does not request an EID pool.
+
+The minimal PLDM responder supports the Base commands SetTID, GetTID,
+GetPLDMVersion, GetPLDMTypes, and GetPLDMCommands. The Platform type
+supports GetSensorReading (``0x11``) for sensor ID 1, GetPDRRepositoryInfo
+(``0x50``), and GetPDR (``0x51``). The reading is a
+signed 32-bit little-endian value in milli-degrees Celsius. Sensor state
+is always enabled and normal, and event generation is disabled.
+
+The PDR repository contains a Terminus Locator, a Numeric Sensor, a Sensor
+Auxiliary Names, and an Entity Auxiliary Names record. The numeric sensor
+uses degrees Celsius with unit modifier -3 and resolution 1.0, so the
+default raw reading of 35000 becomes 35 degrees Celsius. The locator
+tracks the assigned EID and PLDM TID. The entity is named
+``QEMU_MCTP_<7-bit-address>`` and the sensor is named ``Temperature``.
+
+Requests must fit in one MCTP packet, with at most 64 bytes of payload
+including the message-type byte. Fragmented messages, the integrity-check
+message-type bit, and sensor events are not implemented. GetPDR uses PLDM
+multipart transfers, with a final CRC-8, to keep each response within this
+packet limit. Continuation handles identify the record, offset, and
+repository generation; an EID or TID change invalidates old transfers.
+Unsupported commands return the appropriate completion code. Packets
+with invalid PEC, length, destination EID, or tag ownership are dropped.
+
+Guest configuration
+-------------------
+
+Enable these guest kernel options (built-in or as modules):
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   CONFIG_MCTP=y
+   CONFIG_I2C_ASPEED=y
+   CONFIG_I2C_SLAVE=y
+   CONFIG_MCTP_TRANSPORT_I2C=y
+
+In the guest's device tree, enable the local BMC MCTP endpoint on bus 4.
+The address below is the BMC's own I2C address, not the sensor's address:
+
+.. code-block:: dts
+
+   #include <dt-bindings/i2c/i2c.h>
+
+   &i2c4 {
+       status = "okay";
+       multi-master;
+       mctp-controller;
+
+       mctp@10 {
+           compatible = "mctp-i2c-controller";
+           reg = <(0x10 | I2C_OWN_SLAVE_ADDRESS)>;
+       };
+   };
+
+Rebuild and boot the guest DTB, then check the interface name with
+``mctp link``. The following commands assume that the adapter is numbered
+4, producing interface ``mctpi2c4``. Use the actual interface name if
+the guest numbers adapters differently.
+
+If QEMU boots U-Boot from a flash image, U-Boot obtains the device tree
+from the FIT image in flash. Rebuild the kernel FIT and flash image to
+use the updated DTS. For a quick check, keep the MTD drive in the QEMU
+command and boot the kernel, corrected DTB, and OpenBMC initramfs directly:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   -kernel /path/to/linux.bin \
+   -dtb /path/to/aspeed-ast2600-evb-mctp.dtb \
+   -initrd /path/to/obmc-phosphor-initramfs-evb-ast2600.cpio.xz
+
+If ``busctl tree au.com.codeconstruct.MCTP1`` only contains ``interfaces/lo``
+and ``mctp link`` does not list ``mctpi2c4``, the local transport has not
+been created. Seeing ``0x1d`` with ``i2cdetect`` only verifies the remote
+I2C device is present. Ensure the running device tree contains both
+``mctp-controller`` on bus 4 and the ``mctp@10`` local endpoint above.
+Restarting ``mctpd`` alone cannot create a missing kernel interface.
+
+On a device-tree-described adapter, creating an
+``mctp-i2c-interface`` client via ``new_device`` is insufficient if the
+adapter lacks ``mctp-controller``: the driver registers its I2C receiver
+but does not create the MCTP network device. Use the corrected DTB.
+
+With CodeConstruct's ``mctp`` tools installed in the guest, bring up the
+interface and assign the BMC a local EID. Omit the address command if
+EID 8 is already configured:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   mctp addr add 8 dev mctpi2c4
+   mctp link set mctpi2c4 up
+   systemctl restart mctpd
+
+Configure ``role = "bus-owner"`` in ``/etc/mctpd.conf``. To enumerate the
+known physical address and assign an EID, call:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   busctl call au.com.codeconstruct.MCTP1 \
+       /au/com/codeconstruct/mctp1/interfaces/mctpi2c4 \
+       au.com.codeconstruct.MCTP.BusOwner1 SetupEndpoint ay 1 0x1d
+
+``ay 1 0x1d`` is an array containing one byte: the sensor's 7-bit I2C
+address. It is not the shifted on-wire address ``0x3a``. Since there is
+no ARP support, supply this physical address to ``mctpd`` or to the
+platform's endpoint configuration.
+
+On a network with only local EID 8, the default dynamic allocation range
+typically results in:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   yisb 9 1 "/au/com/codeconstruct/mctp1/networks/1/endpoints/9" true
+
+Use the returned EID, network, and object path rather than assuming 9
+is available. The daemon creates the route and neighbour entry. Inspect
+the registered endpoint with:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   busctl introspect au.com.codeconstruct.MCTP1 \
+       /au/com/codeconstruct/mctp1/networks/1/endpoints/9
+   mctp route
+   mctp neigh
+
+Read temperature using ``mctp-client`` (available in CodeConstruct mctp
+2.5). The MCTP type is specified separately; the data starts with the
+three-byte PLDM header:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   mctp-client net 1 eid 9 type pldm data 80 02 11 01 00 00
+
+The default reply is:
+
+.. code-block:: none
+
+   00 02 11 00 05 00 00 01 01 01 B8 88 00 00
+
+The final four bytes encode 35000 (35 degrees Celsius). The preceding
+fields are the PLDM response header, completion code, sensor data size,
+operational state, event enable, present state, previous state, and
+event state.
+
+OpenBMC PLDM sensor discovery
+----------------------------
+
+With OpenBMC's ``pldmd`` installed and running, the daemon discovers the
+MCTP endpoint, retrieves its PDRs, and creates an inventory object and a
+temperature sensor. For the board's device at ``0x1d``, inspect them with:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   busctl tree xyz.openbmc_project.PLDM
+   busctl get-property xyz.openbmc_project.PLDM \
+       /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/temperature/QEMU_MCTP_1d_Temperature \
+       xyz.openbmc_project.Sensor.Value Value
+
+The default value is ``d 35``. Changing the QOM ``temperature`` property
+updates the D-Bus value through ``pldmd``'s sensor polling. The raw PLDM
+reading remains in milli-degrees Celsius; D-Bus reports degrees Celsius.
+For example, a raw value of -12500 is published as -12.5 degrees Celsius.
+Restart ``pldmd`` to repeat discovery if it was started against an older
+QEMU model without PDR support. Event setup and firmware-update discovery
+may log unsupported-operation messages; this sensor uses polling and does
+not implement firmware update.
+
+QOM properties
+--------------
+
+``temperature`` is runtime writable, in milli-degrees Celsius. ``eid``
+is runtime readable and reports the current assigned EID. For the
+board's default sensor, these QMP commands inspect or change them:
+
+.. code-block:: json
+
+   {"execute":"qom-get","arguments":{"path":"/machine/mctp-sensor","property":"eid"}}
+   {"execute":"qom-set","arguments":{"path":"/machine/mctp-sensor","property":"temperature","value":42000}}
+
+Additional devices can be attached at different fixed I2C addresses:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   -device mctp-i2c-sensor,bus=aspeed.i2c.bus.4,address=0x1e,id=temp1,\
+   uuid=8af8149a-76ee-4aa1-b042-19a609ff1d01
+
+Such a device has QOM path ``/machine/peripheral/temp1``. The optional
+``initial-eid`` property defaults to zero; leave it zero to exercise EID
+assignment. ``uuid`` may be supplied for a persistent identity; otherwise
+a UUID is generated once when the device is realized. The default board
+sensor has a fixed UUID.
+
+Reset clears the assigned EID to ``initial-eid`` and restores TID 1.
+Temperature and UUID are retained. Migration preserves these values
+when the device is idle; it is rejected while an I2C request or reply
+is in progress.
+
+Testing
+-------
+
+Build and run the wire-level tests:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+   ninja -C build qemu-system-arm tests/qtest/mctp-i2c-sensor-test
+   QTEST_QEMU_BINARY=./build/qemu-system-arm \
+       ./build/tests/qtest/mctp-i2c-sensor-test
